1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,985
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,165 --> 00:00:05,229
Charlie Munger, Warren Buffett's longtime partner,

3
00:00:05,229 --> 00:00:07,251
used to say the first one hundred

4
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:09,212
thousand dollars is the hardest.

5
00:00:09,392 --> 00:00:11,572
He didn't say it that politely,

6
00:00:11,572 --> 00:00:13,049
but you get the idea.

7
00:00:13,229 --> 00:00:15,284
So today, the real question.

8
00:00:15,464 --> 00:00:17,743
What actually changes when your net worth

9
00:00:17,743 --> 00:00:19,632
hits one hundred thousand dollars?

10
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:21,670
Because something does change.

11
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:23,731
And it's not what most people expect.

12
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,379
One hundred thousand dollars of net worth.

13
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:28,000
That's everything you own,

14
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,718
your savings, your investments,

15
00:00:29,718 --> 00:00:31,048
the equity in your home,

16
00:00:31,048 --> 00:00:32,434
minus everything you owe.

17
00:00:32,614 --> 00:00:34,769
It's the line where money starts behaving

18
00:00:34,769 --> 00:00:35,400
differently.

19
00:00:35,751 --> 00:00:37,388
Here's why that line matters.

20
00:00:37,568 --> 00:00:39,595
Below it, almost all of your progress

21
00:00:39,595 --> 00:00:41,293
comes from what you save out of

22
00:00:41,293 --> 00:00:42,061
your paycheck.

23
00:00:42,241 --> 00:00:44,314
Above it, your money starts doing a

24
00:00:44,314 --> 00:00:45,677
real share of the work.

25
00:00:45,857 --> 00:00:47,598
The growth on your money starts to

26
00:00:47,598 --> 00:00:48,980
rival the money you put in.

27
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:51,102
That's the moment the game changes,

28
00:00:51,102 --> 00:00:53,433
and most people never notice it happening.

29
00:00:53,783 --> 00:00:54,685
In this video,

30
00:00:54,685 --> 00:00:57,456
we'll go through fifteen things that change

31
00:00:57,456 --> 00:00:59,518
at one hundred thousand dollars.

32
00:00:59,698 --> 00:01:00,882
Some are exciting.

33
00:01:01,062 --> 00:01:02,339
Some are dangerous.

34
00:01:02,519 --> 00:01:04,860
And number fifteen is the mistake that

35
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:06,892
keeps a lot of people stuck right

36
00:01:06,892 --> 00:01:08,371
at this level for years.

37
00:01:08,551 --> 00:01:10,695
We'll also look at exactly how long

38
00:01:10,695 --> 00:01:12,043
it takes to get there,

39
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:12,962
with real math.

40
00:01:13,313 --> 00:01:14,381
Let's get into it.

41
00:01:14,561 --> 00:01:16,873
First, where does one hundred thousand dollars

42
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:18,682
put you compared with everyone else?

43
00:01:19,033 --> 00:01:20,986
Every number here comes from a named

44
00:01:20,986 --> 00:01:21,366
source.

45
00:01:21,546 --> 00:01:23,861
Net worth data comes from the Federal

46
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:26,239
Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances,

47
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:28,992
the most detailed look at American household

48
00:01:28,992 --> 00:01:31,620
wealth, last published for the year twenty

49
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:32,309
twenty two.

50
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:34,511
Growth examples use simple,

51
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:37,582
stated math, like a seven percent average

52
00:01:37,582 --> 00:01:38,630
yearly return.

53
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:40,304
That's an illustration,

54
00:01:40,304 --> 00:01:41,214
not a promise.

55
00:01:41,394 --> 00:01:43,286
Markets go down as well as up.

56
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:44,800
This is education,

57
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,357
not financial advice.

58
00:01:46,707 --> 00:01:49,368
According to the Federal Reserve's Survey of

59
00:01:49,368 --> 00:01:51,908
Consumer Finances, the median net worth of

60
00:01:51,908 --> 00:01:54,448
all American families in twenty twenty two

61
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:56,928
was about one hundred ninety two thousand

62
00:01:56,928 --> 00:01:58,259
seven hundred dollars.

63
00:01:58,439 --> 00:02:00,861
Median means half of families had more,

64
00:02:00,861 --> 00:02:01,980
and half had less.

65
00:02:02,330 --> 00:02:04,419
But age changes everything.

66
00:02:04,599 --> 00:02:07,111
For families headed by someone under thirty

67
00:02:07,111 --> 00:02:09,330
five, the median was about thirty nine

68
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:10,323
thousand dollars.

69
00:02:10,503 --> 00:02:12,184
For thirty five to forty four,

70
00:02:12,184 --> 00:02:14,369
about one hundred thirty five thousand.

71
00:02:14,549 --> 00:02:16,364
So if you hit one hundred thousand

72
00:02:16,364 --> 00:02:18,233
in your twenties or early thirties,

73
00:02:18,233 --> 00:02:20,315
you're well ahead of the typical family

74
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:20,795
your age.

75
00:02:21,146 --> 00:02:22,562
And look at the averages.

76
00:02:22,742 --> 00:02:25,113
The average net worth for all families

77
00:02:25,113 --> 00:02:26,922
was over one million dollars.

78
00:02:27,102 --> 00:02:29,021
That's far above the median,

79
00:02:29,021 --> 00:02:31,627
because a small number of very wealthy

80
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:33,615
families pull the average up.

81
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:35,606
That gap is a reminder.

82
00:02:35,786 --> 00:02:38,208
When people talk about what the average

83
00:02:38,208 --> 00:02:40,631
American has, ask whether they mean the

84
00:02:40,631 --> 00:02:41,997
average or the median.

85
00:02:42,177 --> 00:02:44,290
The median is the better picture of

86
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:45,498
a typical household.

87
00:02:45,848 --> 00:02:48,406
There's another reason this line matters,

88
00:02:48,406 --> 00:02:49,841
and it's psychological.

89
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:51,797
Before one hundred thousand,

90
00:02:51,797 --> 00:02:54,335
many people feel like they're not really

91
00:02:54,335 --> 00:02:55,223
investors yet.

92
00:02:55,403 --> 00:02:56,866
Just people saving a bit.

93
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,530
After it, they start thinking like owners.

94
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:01,194
Owners of businesses,

95
00:03:01,194 --> 00:03:02,183
through funds.

96
00:03:02,363 --> 00:03:04,128
Owners of a growing machine.

97
00:03:04,308 --> 00:03:06,827
That shift in identity changes behavior.

98
00:03:07,007 --> 00:03:09,899
People who see themselves as investors tend

99
00:03:09,899 --> 00:03:11,513
to stay invested longer,

100
00:03:11,513 --> 00:03:13,799
panic less, and keep adding money.

101
00:03:13,979 --> 00:03:14,884
In many ways,

102
00:03:14,884 --> 00:03:16,974
the six figure mark is as much

103
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:19,412
a mindset milestone as a money one.

104
00:03:19,763 --> 00:03:20,540
Alright.

105
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:22,462
Now the fifteen changes.

106
00:03:22,642 --> 00:03:24,544
Number one is the reason Munger said

107
00:03:24,544 --> 00:03:26,763
the first hundred thousand is the hardest.

108
00:03:27,114 --> 00:03:28,228
Change number one.

109
00:03:28,408 --> 00:03:30,926
Compounding stops being a theory and becomes

110
00:03:30,926 --> 00:03:33,215
something you can see on your statement.

111
00:03:33,565 --> 00:03:34,967
At small balances,

112
00:03:34,967 --> 00:03:37,071
growth is almost invisible.

113
00:03:37,251 --> 00:03:39,700
Seven percent of five thousand dollars is

114
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:41,790
three hundred fifty dollars a year.

115
00:03:41,970 --> 00:03:44,025
Nice, but it doesn't move the needle.

116
00:03:44,205 --> 00:03:46,891
Seven percent of one hundred thousand dollars

117
00:03:46,891 --> 00:03:48,861
is seven thousand dollars a year.

118
00:03:49,041 --> 00:03:50,237
Same percentage.

119
00:03:50,417 --> 00:03:52,031
Twenty times the result.

120
00:03:52,211 --> 00:03:54,214
That's why the early years feel so

121
00:03:54,214 --> 00:03:56,335
slow, and why it suddenly feels like

122
00:03:56,335 --> 00:03:57,690
things are speeding up.

123
00:03:58,041 --> 00:03:59,608
Picture it like a snowball.

124
00:03:59,788 --> 00:04:01,107
At the top of the hill,

125
00:04:01,107 --> 00:04:03,114
it's small and you're doing all the

126
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:03,573
pushing.

127
00:04:03,753 --> 00:04:05,698
Around one hundred thousand dollars,

128
00:04:05,698 --> 00:04:07,698
it's heavy enough to start rolling on

129
00:04:07,698 --> 00:04:08,130
its own.

130
00:04:08,310 --> 00:04:09,304
You still push,

131
00:04:09,304 --> 00:04:10,829
but now the hill helps.

132
00:04:11,009 --> 00:04:12,785
Try this: look at your last year

133
00:04:12,785 --> 00:04:15,394
of investment statements and separate the money

134
00:04:15,394 --> 00:04:16,837
you added from the growth.

135
00:04:17,017 --> 00:04:19,198
Watching that growth line catch up with

136
00:04:19,198 --> 00:04:21,267
your contributions is one of the most

137
00:04:21,267 --> 00:04:23,391
motivating things in personal finance.

138
00:04:23,742 --> 00:04:25,088
Here's the math on screen.

139
00:04:25,268 --> 00:04:27,080
One hundred thousand dollars,

140
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,267
with nothing added,

141
00:04:28,267 --> 00:04:30,765
at a seven percent average yearly return

142
00:04:30,765 --> 00:04:34,263
compounded monthly, becomes roughly two hundred thousand

143
00:04:34,263 --> 00:04:35,450
in about ten years.

144
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:37,503
That's the rule of seventy two in

145
00:04:37,503 --> 00:04:39,262
action, which we'll get to in a

146
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:39,659
moment.

147
00:04:40,009 --> 00:04:41,193
Change number two.

148
00:04:41,373 --> 00:04:43,417
Your money can start earning about as

149
00:04:43,417 --> 00:04:44,357
much as you save.

150
00:04:44,707 --> 00:04:46,610
Say you save five hundred dollars a

151
00:04:46,610 --> 00:04:46,936
month.

152
00:04:47,116 --> 00:04:49,089
That's six thousand dollars a year.

153
00:04:49,269 --> 00:04:50,756
At a seven percent return,

154
00:04:50,756 --> 00:04:53,328
one hundred thousand dollars would earn about

155
00:04:53,328 --> 00:04:55,214
seven thousand dollars in a year.

156
00:04:55,394 --> 00:04:56,292
In a good year,

157
00:04:56,292 --> 00:04:58,209
your money puts in more than you

158
00:04:58,209 --> 00:04:58,389
do.

159
00:04:58,569 --> 00:05:01,030
That's a strange and wonderful feeling the

160
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:02,319
first time it happens.

161
00:05:02,669 --> 00:05:04,722
This is exactly why the first one

162
00:05:04,722 --> 00:05:06,465
hundred thousand is so hard.

163
00:05:06,645 --> 00:05:08,270
Before it, you are the engine.

164
00:05:08,450 --> 00:05:10,610
After it, you have a second engine.

165
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:11,656
In a bad year,

166
00:05:11,656 --> 00:05:13,884
of course, the market can take money

167
00:05:13,884 --> 00:05:16,111
away instead, and that's part of the

168
00:05:16,111 --> 00:05:16,421
deal.

169
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:18,100
But over long periods,

170
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:20,827
the second engine tends to get stronger.

171
00:05:21,007 --> 00:05:23,581
Try this: write two numbers at the

172
00:05:23,581 --> 00:05:24,641
top of a page.

173
00:05:24,821 --> 00:05:26,107
What you saved last year,

174
00:05:26,107 --> 00:05:27,804
and what your investments earned.

175
00:05:27,984 --> 00:05:29,494
Track both every year.

176
00:05:29,844 --> 00:05:31,156
Change number three.

177
00:05:31,336 --> 00:05:33,395
The rule of seventy two starts to

178
00:05:33,395 --> 00:05:33,832
matter.

179
00:05:34,182 --> 00:05:36,150
The rule of seventy two is a

180
00:05:36,150 --> 00:05:36,782
shortcut.

181
00:05:36,962 --> 00:05:39,255
Divide seventy two by your yearly return,

182
00:05:39,255 --> 00:05:41,157
and you get roughly how many years

183
00:05:41,157 --> 00:05:42,779
it takes for money to double.

184
00:05:42,959 --> 00:05:44,227
At seven percent,

185
00:05:44,227 --> 00:05:45,943
that's about ten years.

186
00:05:46,123 --> 00:05:47,236
At ten percent,

187
00:05:47,236 --> 00:05:48,572
about seven years.

188
00:05:48,752 --> 00:05:49,955
At two percent,

189
00:05:49,955 --> 00:05:51,318
thirty six years.

190
00:05:51,498 --> 00:05:52,733
It's an estimate,

191
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:54,259
not an exact formula,

192
00:05:54,259 --> 00:05:56,293
but it's surprisingly close.

193
00:05:56,643 --> 00:05:58,694
When you have five thousand dollars,

194
00:05:58,694 --> 00:06:01,087
doubling to ten thousand doesn't feel life

195
00:06:01,087 --> 00:06:01,600
changing.

196
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:03,831
When you have one hundred thousand,

197
00:06:03,831 --> 00:06:06,703
doubling to two hundred thousand absolutely does.

198
00:06:06,883 --> 00:06:08,555
And the next double after that is

199
00:06:08,555 --> 00:06:09,669
four hundred thousand.

200
00:06:09,849 --> 00:06:12,213
That's why patience is the most powerful

201
00:06:12,213 --> 00:06:13,158
financial skill.

202
00:06:13,338 --> 00:06:15,570
Try this: use the rule of seventy

203
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:18,006
two on your own savings account rate

204
00:06:18,006 --> 00:06:18,412
today.

205
00:06:18,592 --> 00:06:20,234
If the answer is a very big

206
00:06:20,234 --> 00:06:22,727
number, your money is probably sitting in

207
00:06:22,727 --> 00:06:23,700
the wrong place.

208
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:25,420
Change number four.

209
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,358
You start tracking net worth,

210
00:06:27,358 --> 00:06:28,328
not just income.

211
00:06:28,678 --> 00:06:30,640
Income is what comes in.

212
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:32,190
Net worth is what you keep.

213
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:34,694
Lots of people with big salaries have

214
00:06:34,694 --> 00:06:35,825
a small net worth,

215
00:06:35,825 --> 00:06:38,526
because everything that comes in goes right

216
00:06:38,526 --> 00:06:39,092
back out.

217
00:06:39,272 --> 00:06:41,961
Lots of people with ordinary salaries build

218
00:06:41,961 --> 00:06:44,338
real wealth, because they keep the gap

219
00:06:44,338 --> 00:06:46,152
between earning and spending,

220
00:06:46,152 --> 00:06:47,027
and invest it.

221
00:06:47,207 --> 00:06:48,734
At one hundred thousand,

222
00:06:48,734 --> 00:06:51,215
net worth becomes the number that tells

223
00:06:51,215 --> 00:06:51,851
the truth.

224
00:06:52,202 --> 00:06:53,839
Here's a simple example.

225
00:06:54,019 --> 00:06:56,593
Two people each earn eighty thousand dollars

226
00:06:56,593 --> 00:06:57,002
a year.

227
00:06:57,182 --> 00:06:59,226
One spends seventy eight thousand.

228
00:06:59,406 --> 00:07:02,070
The other spends sixty five thousand and

229
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:03,202
invests the rest.

230
00:07:03,382 --> 00:07:04,325
After ten years,

231
00:07:04,325 --> 00:07:06,505
their incomes look the same on paper.

232
00:07:06,685 --> 00:07:08,775
Their net worth looks completely different.

233
00:07:08,955 --> 00:07:10,447
Try this: once a month,

234
00:07:10,447 --> 00:07:12,523
write down what you own and what

235
00:07:12,523 --> 00:07:13,042
you owe.

236
00:07:13,222 --> 00:07:14,058
One number.

237
00:07:14,238 --> 00:07:15,547
It takes ten minutes,

238
00:07:15,547 --> 00:07:17,543
and it changes how you see every

239
00:07:17,543 --> 00:07:18,104
purchase.

240
00:07:18,454 --> 00:07:19,707
Change number five.

241
00:07:19,887 --> 00:07:21,199
Fees start to hurt.

242
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:24,092
A one percent yearly fee sounds tiny.

243
00:07:24,272 --> 00:07:26,107
On one hundred thousand dollars,

244
00:07:26,107 --> 00:07:27,999
it's one thousand dollars a year.

245
00:07:28,179 --> 00:07:28,864
Every year.

246
00:07:29,044 --> 00:07:31,562
And that thousand dollars would otherwise have

247
00:07:31,562 --> 00:07:32,492
kept compounding.

248
00:07:32,672 --> 00:07:35,202
Over decades, a one percent difference in

249
00:07:35,202 --> 00:07:36,744
fees can add up to a very

250
00:07:36,744 --> 00:07:38,102
large amount of money,

251
00:07:38,102 --> 00:07:40,138
because you lose both the fee and

252
00:07:40,138 --> 00:07:42,483
all the growth it would have produced.

253
00:07:42,833 --> 00:07:46,119
Funds charge something called an expense ratio,

254
00:07:46,119 --> 00:07:48,847
a yearly percentage of what you invest.

255
00:07:49,027 --> 00:07:51,924
Some broad index funds charge a small

256
00:07:51,924 --> 00:07:53,647
fraction of a percent.

257
00:07:53,827 --> 00:07:56,836
Some actively managed funds and some advice

258
00:07:56,836 --> 00:07:58,936
arrangements charge much more.

259
00:07:59,116 --> 00:08:00,718
Higher fees aren't always wrong,

260
00:08:00,718 --> 00:08:02,672
but you should know exactly what you're

261
00:08:02,672 --> 00:08:04,224
paying and what you get for it.

262
00:08:04,404 --> 00:08:07,369
Try this: find the expense ratio of

263
00:08:07,369 --> 00:08:08,978
every fund you own.

264
00:08:09,158 --> 00:08:11,109
It's listed on the fund's page.

265
00:08:11,289 --> 00:08:12,800
Add them up in dollars,

266
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:13,588
not percent.

267
00:08:13,938 --> 00:08:15,203
Let's put a number on that.

268
00:08:15,383 --> 00:08:18,332
Imagine one hundred thousand dollars growing for

269
00:08:18,332 --> 00:08:20,544
twenty years at seven percent a year

270
00:08:20,544 --> 00:08:21,281
before fees.

271
00:08:21,461 --> 00:08:23,430
If the total yearly cost is a

272
00:08:23,430 --> 00:08:25,331
small fraction of a percent,

273
00:08:25,331 --> 00:08:27,707
you keep almost all of that growth.

274
00:08:27,887 --> 00:08:30,035
If the cost is around one percent,

275
00:08:30,035 --> 00:08:32,750
your return effectively drops to around six

276
00:08:32,750 --> 00:08:35,150
percent, and the ending balance can be

277
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:37,361
tens of thousands of dollars lower.

278
00:08:37,541 --> 00:08:38,423
Same market.

279
00:08:38,603 --> 00:08:39,578
Same years.

280
00:08:39,758 --> 00:08:41,522
The only difference is what you paid

281
00:08:41,522 --> 00:08:42,208
along the way.

282
00:08:42,388 --> 00:08:45,028
That's why low cost investing became so

283
00:08:45,028 --> 00:08:45,569
popular.

284
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:47,522
Okay, quick check.

285
00:08:47,702 --> 00:08:49,619
If you're finding this useful,

286
00:08:49,619 --> 00:08:51,665
the next few get more practical,

287
00:08:51,665 --> 00:08:53,135
especially number nine.

288
00:08:53,315 --> 00:08:54,325
Let's keep going.

289
00:08:54,676 --> 00:08:56,046
Change number six.

290
00:08:56,226 --> 00:08:58,948
Your mix of investments matters more than

291
00:08:58,948 --> 00:09:00,010
any single pick.

292
00:09:00,361 --> 00:09:01,295
At this level,

293
00:09:01,295 --> 00:09:03,829
the big decision isn't which hot stock

294
00:09:03,829 --> 00:09:04,296
to buy.

295
00:09:04,476 --> 00:09:06,948
It's how your money is split between

296
00:09:06,948 --> 00:09:09,008
broad categories, like stocks,

297
00:09:09,008 --> 00:09:10,038
bonds and cash.

298
00:09:10,218 --> 00:09:12,412
That mix, called asset allocation,

299
00:09:12,412 --> 00:09:15,123
drives most of how your portfolio behaves,

300
00:09:15,123 --> 00:09:16,479
how much it can grow,

301
00:09:16,479 --> 00:09:18,157
and how far it can fall in

302
00:09:18,157 --> 00:09:18,867
a bad year.

303
00:09:19,217 --> 00:09:21,462
Someone in their twenties with a stable

304
00:09:21,462 --> 00:09:23,131
job might hold mostly stocks,

305
00:09:23,131 --> 00:09:25,261
because they have decades to wait out

306
00:09:25,261 --> 00:09:26,066
the bad years.

307
00:09:26,246 --> 00:09:29,290
Someone close to retirement usually holds more

308
00:09:29,290 --> 00:09:30,283
bonds and cash,

309
00:09:30,283 --> 00:09:32,400
because they can't wait as long.

310
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:34,602
There's no single right answer,

311
00:09:34,602 --> 00:09:36,233
but there is a wrong one:

312
00:09:36,233 --> 00:09:37,537
not choosing at all.

313
00:09:37,717 --> 00:09:40,222
Try this: open your accounts and write

314
00:09:40,222 --> 00:09:41,607
down your actual mix.

315
00:09:41,787 --> 00:09:43,890
Many people are surprised by what they

316
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:44,167
find.

317
00:09:44,517 --> 00:09:45,759
Change number seven.

318
00:09:45,939 --> 00:09:48,307
Market drops start to feel real.

319
00:09:48,658 --> 00:09:51,322
When you had five thousand dollars invested,

320
00:09:51,322 --> 00:09:53,260
a twenty percent drop cost you a

321
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:54,834
thousand dollars on paper.

322
00:09:55,014 --> 00:09:56,895
Painful, but survivable.

323
00:09:57,075 --> 00:09:59,117
With one hundred thousand dollars,

324
00:09:59,117 --> 00:10:01,580
the same drop is twenty thousand dollars.

325
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:03,199
That can be more than you saved

326
00:10:03,199 --> 00:10:03,942
in a whole year.

327
00:10:04,122 --> 00:10:06,203
And that's when many people make their

328
00:10:06,203 --> 00:10:08,394
worst decision: they sell after the drop

329
00:10:08,394 --> 00:10:09,544
and lock in the loss.

330
00:10:09,894 --> 00:10:12,283
Markets have always had bad years,

331
00:10:12,283 --> 00:10:13,899
and some very bad ones.

332
00:10:14,079 --> 00:10:17,634
Historically, broad markets have recovered over long

333
00:10:17,634 --> 00:10:20,164
periods, but there are no guarantees,

334
00:10:20,164 --> 00:10:22,078
and recovery can take years.

335
00:10:22,258 --> 00:10:24,858
The best protection is deciding your plan

336
00:10:24,858 --> 00:10:26,380
before the drop happens.

337
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:28,493
Try this: write a short note to

338
00:10:28,493 --> 00:10:29,927
your future self today.

339
00:10:30,107 --> 00:10:32,853
When my portfolio falls twenty percent,

340
00:10:32,853 --> 00:10:33,346
I will.

341
00:10:33,526 --> 00:10:34,919
Then finish the sentence.

342
00:10:35,099 --> 00:10:36,608
Read it when it happens.

343
00:10:36,959 --> 00:10:38,868
Another way to handle drops is to

344
00:10:38,868 --> 00:10:40,488
keep investing through them.

345
00:10:40,668 --> 00:10:42,870
When you invest the same amount every

346
00:10:42,870 --> 00:10:45,548
month, you automatically buy more shares when

347
00:10:45,548 --> 00:10:47,691
prices are low and fewer when prices

348
00:10:47,691 --> 00:10:48,226
are high.

349
00:10:48,406 --> 00:10:50,925
It's called dollar cost averaging.

350
00:10:51,105 --> 00:10:53,645
It doesn't guarantee a profit or protect

351
00:10:53,645 --> 00:10:56,502
against losses, but it removes the impossible

352
00:10:56,502 --> 00:10:58,724
job of guessing the perfect moment,

353
00:10:58,724 --> 00:11:00,628
and it turns a scary drop into

354
00:11:00,628 --> 00:11:02,596
a sale on what you were already

355
00:11:02,596 --> 00:11:03,041
buying.

356
00:11:03,391 --> 00:11:04,703
Change number eight.

357
00:11:04,883 --> 00:11:07,135
Taxes start to matter a lot more.

358
00:11:07,485 --> 00:11:10,149
Retirement accounts like a four oh one

359
00:11:10,149 --> 00:11:11,832
k or an IRA give you tax

360
00:11:11,832 --> 00:11:14,075
advantages, either now or later,

361
00:11:14,075 --> 00:11:17,020
in exchange for keeping the money invested

362
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:18,282
for the long term.

363
00:11:18,462 --> 00:11:20,391
At one hundred thousand dollars,

364
00:11:20,391 --> 00:11:23,646
the difference between investing inside those accounts

365
00:11:23,646 --> 00:11:25,816
and outside them can be thousands of

366
00:11:25,816 --> 00:11:27,866
dollars a year in taxes over time.

367
00:11:28,046 --> 00:11:30,082
The rules and limits change,

368
00:11:30,082 --> 00:11:32,991
so always check the current numbers with

369
00:11:32,991 --> 00:11:33,572
the IRS.

370
00:11:33,923 --> 00:11:36,337
A common order many people follow:

371
00:11:36,337 --> 00:11:38,752
first, get any employer match on a

372
00:11:38,752 --> 00:11:39,746
four oh one k,

373
00:11:39,746 --> 00:11:42,444
because that's extra money for saving.

374
00:11:42,624 --> 00:11:44,761
Next, consider an IRA.

375
00:11:44,941 --> 00:11:47,058
Then, if you still have money to

376
00:11:47,058 --> 00:11:49,902
invest, add more to tax advantaged accounts

377
00:11:49,902 --> 00:11:51,953
or a regular brokerage account.

378
00:11:52,133 --> 00:11:54,595
Try this: check whether your employer offers

379
00:11:54,595 --> 00:11:56,778
a match, and whether you're getting all

380
00:11:56,778 --> 00:11:57,114
of it.

381
00:11:57,294 --> 00:11:59,048
Leaving a match on the table is

382
00:11:59,048 --> 00:12:00,858
leaving part of your pay behind.

383
00:12:01,208 --> 00:12:02,473
Change number nine.

384
00:12:02,653 --> 00:12:04,963
Your emergency fund becomes the guard dog

385
00:12:04,963 --> 00:12:06,090
of your investments.

386
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:07,264
Here's why.

387
00:12:07,444 --> 00:12:09,729
If you have investments but no cash

388
00:12:09,729 --> 00:12:12,472
cushion, any emergency forces you to sell,

389
00:12:12,472 --> 00:12:14,626
maybe at the worst possible time,

390
00:12:14,626 --> 00:12:16,651
maybe with taxes and penalties.

391
00:12:16,831 --> 00:12:18,777
A cash cushion of a few months

392
00:12:18,777 --> 00:12:21,697
of expenses means your investments never have

393
00:12:21,697 --> 00:12:23,643
to be touched for a broken car

394
00:12:23,643 --> 00:12:24,551
or a lost job.

395
00:12:24,731 --> 00:12:26,856
The cushion protects the compounding.

396
00:12:27,206 --> 00:12:28,727
Think of it as two walls.

397
00:12:28,907 --> 00:12:31,554
The outer wall is cash for emergencies.

398
00:12:31,734 --> 00:12:33,633
The inner wall is your long term

399
00:12:33,633 --> 00:12:34,346
investments.

400
00:12:34,526 --> 00:12:36,495
As long as the outer wall holds,

401
00:12:36,495 --> 00:12:38,218
the inner one keeps growing.

402
00:12:38,398 --> 00:12:40,248
If you haven't watched my video on

403
00:12:40,248 --> 00:12:42,425
what changes at twenty thousand dollars,

404
00:12:42,425 --> 00:12:43,622
that's the outer wall.

405
00:12:43,802 --> 00:12:45,962
Try this: check how many months of

406
00:12:45,962 --> 00:12:48,249
expenses you hold in cash right now.

407
00:12:48,429 --> 00:12:49,740
If it's less than three,

408
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:52,034
building that up may come before investing

409
00:12:52,034 --> 00:12:52,307
more.

410
00:12:52,657 --> 00:12:54,700
Many people use a simple target.

411
00:12:54,880 --> 00:12:57,749
Three months of essential expenses if their

412
00:12:57,749 --> 00:12:58,883
income is stable,

413
00:12:58,883 --> 00:13:01,152
six months or more if their income

414
00:13:01,152 --> 00:13:03,553
is irregular, or if they're the only

415
00:13:03,553 --> 00:13:04,888
earner in the house.

416
00:13:05,068 --> 00:13:07,447
At one hundred thousand of net worth,

417
00:13:07,447 --> 00:13:09,826
that might mean keeping ten or twenty

418
00:13:09,826 --> 00:13:12,012
thousand in cash while the rest is

419
00:13:12,012 --> 00:13:12,591
invested.

420
00:13:12,771 --> 00:13:14,838
It can feel like money doing nothing.

421
00:13:15,018 --> 00:13:15,645
It isn't.

422
00:13:15,825 --> 00:13:18,100
It's insurance for the compounding machine.

423
00:13:18,450 --> 00:13:19,680
Change number ten.

424
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:21,816
If part of your net worth is

425
00:13:21,816 --> 00:13:24,329
your home, you realize equity is not

426
00:13:24,329 --> 00:13:24,678
cash.

427
00:13:25,029 --> 00:13:26,971
Net worth includes home equity,

428
00:13:26,971 --> 00:13:29,039
the value of your home minus what

429
00:13:29,039 --> 00:13:29,916
you owe on it.

430
00:13:30,096 --> 00:13:31,513
That's real wealth.

431
00:13:31,693 --> 00:13:33,516
But you can't buy groceries with it.

432
00:13:33,696 --> 00:13:34,482
To use it,

433
00:13:34,482 --> 00:13:35,819
you have to sell,

434
00:13:35,819 --> 00:13:37,234
borrow against it,

435
00:13:37,234 --> 00:13:37,864
or wait.

436
00:13:38,044 --> 00:13:39,826
So two people with the same one

437
00:13:39,826 --> 00:13:42,241
hundred thousand dollar net worth can live

438
00:13:42,241 --> 00:13:44,023
very different financial lives,

439
00:13:44,023 --> 00:13:45,978
depending on how much is locked in

440
00:13:45,978 --> 00:13:46,438
a house.

441
00:13:46,788 --> 00:13:49,514
Someone with one hundred thousand in home

442
00:13:49,514 --> 00:13:51,975
equity and nothing else is house rich

443
00:13:51,975 --> 00:13:52,906
and cash poor.

444
00:13:53,086 --> 00:13:55,686
Someone with fifty thousand in equity,

445
00:13:55,686 --> 00:13:58,901
thirty thousand invested and twenty thousand in

446
00:13:58,901 --> 00:14:00,679
cash has far more options.

447
00:14:00,859 --> 00:14:01,814
Neither is wrong,

448
00:14:01,814 --> 00:14:03,611
but the second is more flexible.

449
00:14:03,791 --> 00:14:06,220
Try this: split your net worth into

450
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:07,192
three buckets.

451
00:14:07,372 --> 00:14:09,659
Cash, investments, and home.

452
00:14:09,839 --> 00:14:12,231
Look at how balanced it really is.

453
00:14:12,581 --> 00:14:14,229
We're past the halfway point.

454
00:14:14,409 --> 00:14:16,866
The last five are about protecting what

455
00:14:16,866 --> 00:14:19,133
you built, and number fifteen is the

456
00:14:19,133 --> 00:14:19,448
trap.

457
00:14:19,628 --> 00:14:20,568
Stay with me.

458
00:14:20,919 --> 00:14:22,289
Change number eleven.

459
00:14:22,469 --> 00:14:24,765
High interest debt becomes an emergency,

460
00:14:24,765 --> 00:14:25,627
not a footnote.

461
00:14:25,977 --> 00:14:28,066
It's hard to find an investment that

462
00:14:28,066 --> 00:14:30,214
reliably beats twenty percent a year.

463
00:14:30,394 --> 00:14:33,205
But credit card interest regularly costs that

464
00:14:33,205 --> 00:14:33,517
much.

465
00:14:33,697 --> 00:14:35,793
So if you have one hundred thousand

466
00:14:35,793 --> 00:14:38,008
dollars of net worth and ten thousand

467
00:14:38,008 --> 00:14:39,445
dollars on credit cards,

468
00:14:39,445 --> 00:14:41,420
paying off the cards is often one

469
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:43,636
of the best returns available to you.

470
00:14:43,816 --> 00:14:45,871
Guaranteed, and tax free.

471
00:14:46,221 --> 00:14:48,009
Low interest debt is different.

472
00:14:48,189 --> 00:14:49,982
A mortgage at a low fixed rate

473
00:14:49,982 --> 00:14:51,716
might not need to be paid off

474
00:14:51,716 --> 00:14:54,168
early, because your money could earn more

475
00:14:54,168 --> 00:14:55,364
elsewhere over time.

476
00:14:55,544 --> 00:14:56,679
The rule of thumb:

477
00:14:56,679 --> 00:14:57,940
the higher the rate,

478
00:14:57,940 --> 00:14:59,642
the more urgent the payoff.

479
00:14:59,822 --> 00:15:01,971
Try this: list every debt with its

480
00:15:01,971 --> 00:15:04,373
interest rate, from highest to lowest.

481
00:15:04,553 --> 00:15:06,204
Attack the top one first,

482
00:15:06,204 --> 00:15:08,976
while making minimum payments on the rest.

483
00:15:09,327 --> 00:15:10,697
Change number twelve.

484
00:15:10,877 --> 00:15:12,781
You become a target for salespeople.

485
00:15:13,131 --> 00:15:15,060
At one hundred thousand dollars,

486
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:16,567
you start getting offers.

487
00:15:16,747 --> 00:15:18,756
Complex insurance products.

488
00:15:18,936 --> 00:15:20,851
Expensive managed accounts.

489
00:15:21,031 --> 00:15:22,773
Exclusive opportunities.

490
00:15:22,953 --> 00:15:24,056
Some are fine.

491
00:15:24,236 --> 00:15:26,048
Many come with high fees and long

492
00:15:26,048 --> 00:15:28,189
lock ups that mainly benefit the person

493
00:15:28,189 --> 00:15:28,903
selling them.

494
00:15:29,083 --> 00:15:31,126
And scammers know this level too.

495
00:15:31,306 --> 00:15:34,464
The Federal Reserve's latest survey found twenty

496
00:15:34,464 --> 00:15:37,622
percent of adults experienced financial fraud or

497
00:15:37,622 --> 00:15:39,201
a scam in the past year.

498
00:15:39,551 --> 00:15:41,626
One simple rule protects you from most

499
00:15:41,626 --> 00:15:41,954
of it.

500
00:15:42,134 --> 00:15:44,151
If you can't explain how a product

501
00:15:44,151 --> 00:15:46,347
makes money, and how the seller makes

502
00:15:46,347 --> 00:15:47,771
money, in two sentences,

503
00:15:47,771 --> 00:15:48,543
don't buy it.

504
00:15:48,723 --> 00:15:50,021
Ask about every fee,

505
00:15:50,021 --> 00:15:52,554
ask whether the advisor is a fiduciary,

506
00:15:52,554 --> 00:15:55,086
meaning legally required to act in your

507
00:15:55,086 --> 00:15:57,554
interest, and never decide on the same

508
00:15:57,554 --> 00:15:57,813
day.

509
00:15:57,993 --> 00:16:00,870
Try this: next time someone pitches you,

510
00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:02,812
ask, how do you get paid on

511
00:16:02,812 --> 00:16:03,171
this?

512
00:16:03,351 --> 00:16:04,814
Their answer tells you a lot.

513
00:16:05,164 --> 00:16:06,754
Change number thirteen.

514
00:16:06,934 --> 00:16:09,163
The boring paperwork starts to matter.

515
00:16:09,514 --> 00:16:12,506
Retirement accounts and many investment accounts let

516
00:16:12,506 --> 00:16:13,829
you name a beneficiary,

517
00:16:13,829 --> 00:16:15,900
the person who receives the money if

518
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:17,339
something happens to you.

519
00:16:17,519 --> 00:16:19,858
Those forms often override what's in a

520
00:16:19,858 --> 00:16:20,166
will.

521
00:16:20,346 --> 00:16:22,101
At one hundred thousand dollars,

522
00:16:22,101 --> 00:16:24,405
getting this wrong can cause real problems

523
00:16:24,405 --> 00:16:25,721
for the people you love.

524
00:16:25,901 --> 00:16:27,712
It's ten minutes of work that most

525
00:16:27,712 --> 00:16:28,351
people skip.

526
00:16:28,701 --> 00:16:30,802
Also consider basic protection.

527
00:16:30,982 --> 00:16:32,979
If people depend on your income,

528
00:16:32,979 --> 00:16:35,288
term life insurance is often a simple

529
00:16:35,288 --> 00:16:37,472
and affordable way to protect them.

530
00:16:37,652 --> 00:16:39,507
And a basic will makes your wishes

531
00:16:39,507 --> 00:16:39,835
clear.

532
00:16:40,015 --> 00:16:41,378
Laws differ by state,

533
00:16:41,378 --> 00:16:43,521
so a local professional can help.

534
00:16:43,701 --> 00:16:46,177
Try this: log into every account this

535
00:16:46,177 --> 00:16:48,252
week and check the beneficiary.

536
00:16:48,432 --> 00:16:50,231
Make sure it's the right person,

537
00:16:50,231 --> 00:16:52,087
and that the information is up to

538
00:16:52,087 --> 00:16:52,368
date.

539
00:16:52,718 --> 00:16:54,308
Change number fourteen.

540
00:16:54,488 --> 00:16:56,090
Your options multiply.

541
00:16:56,441 --> 00:16:58,628
With a solid cushion and one hundred

542
00:16:58,628 --> 00:17:00,450
thousand dollars of net worth,

543
00:17:00,450 --> 00:17:03,063
you can consider moves that were impossible

544
00:17:03,063 --> 00:17:03,488
before.

545
00:17:03,668 --> 00:17:05,461
A lower paying job you love,

546
00:17:05,461 --> 00:17:07,894
because your money is already working.

547
00:17:08,074 --> 00:17:09,892
A career change with a year of

548
00:17:09,892 --> 00:17:10,559
retraining.

549
00:17:10,739 --> 00:17:12,294
A move to a cheaper city.

550
00:17:12,474 --> 00:17:13,867
A small business.

551
00:17:14,047 --> 00:17:15,963
You're not forced to take the first

552
00:17:15,963 --> 00:17:16,729
offer anymore.

553
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:19,135
This is the real point of wealth.

554
00:17:19,315 --> 00:17:20,034
Not stuff.

555
00:17:20,214 --> 00:17:20,946
Choices.

556
00:17:21,126 --> 00:17:23,766
The person with options negotiates better,

557
00:17:23,766 --> 00:17:25,212
waits for better deals,

558
00:17:25,212 --> 00:17:27,349
and recovers faster from bad luck.

559
00:17:27,529 --> 00:17:29,872
Try this: write down one decision you'd

560
00:17:29,872 --> 00:17:32,635
make differently if money weren't the deciding

561
00:17:32,635 --> 00:17:33,055
factor.

562
00:17:33,235 --> 00:17:34,841
Then ask how far you are from

563
00:17:34,841 --> 00:17:35,894
making it possible.

564
00:17:36,074 --> 00:17:38,358
Sometimes the answer is closer than you

565
00:17:38,358 --> 00:17:38,709
think.

566
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:41,173
And now, number fifteen.

567
00:17:41,353 --> 00:17:43,682
The trap that keeps people stuck right

568
00:17:43,682 --> 00:17:43,988
here.

569
00:17:44,338 --> 00:17:45,870
Change number fifteen.

570
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:47,107
Complacency.

571
00:17:47,287 --> 00:17:49,728
Hitting one hundred thousand feels like the

572
00:17:49,728 --> 00:17:50,410
finish line.

573
00:17:50,590 --> 00:17:51,321
It's not.

574
00:17:51,501 --> 00:17:52,720
It's the starting line.

575
00:17:53,071 --> 00:17:54,417
Here's how it happens.

576
00:17:54,597 --> 00:17:55,696
You hit the goal,

577
00:17:55,696 --> 00:17:59,186
you celebrate, and the automatic contributions quietly

578
00:17:59,186 --> 00:17:59,961
get smaller.

579
00:18:00,141 --> 00:18:02,016
A bigger car payment here,

580
00:18:02,016 --> 00:18:03,891
a lifestyle upgrade there.

581
00:18:04,071 --> 00:18:06,087
The snowball is still rolling,

582
00:18:06,087 --> 00:18:08,170
but now you've stopped pushing.

583
00:18:08,350 --> 00:18:10,901
And the years between one hundred thousand

584
00:18:10,901 --> 00:18:13,391
and five hundred thousand are exactly the

585
00:18:13,391 --> 00:18:15,396
years where pushing matters most,

586
00:18:15,396 --> 00:18:17,826
because every dollar you add has decades

587
00:18:17,826 --> 00:18:18,555
to compound.

588
00:18:18,905 --> 00:18:19,973
Look at the difference.

589
00:18:20,153 --> 00:18:21,973
One hundred thousand dollars,

590
00:18:21,973 --> 00:18:24,106
plus five hundred dollars a month,

591
00:18:24,106 --> 00:18:26,679
at a seven percent average yearly return,

592
00:18:26,679 --> 00:18:27,746
for twenty years.

593
00:18:27,926 --> 00:18:29,690
That's the kind of path that can

594
00:18:29,690 --> 00:18:31,896
turn one hundred thousand into far more.

595
00:18:32,076 --> 00:18:34,990
Compare that with stopping contributions entirely.

596
00:18:35,341 --> 00:18:36,595
On top of that chart,

597
00:18:36,595 --> 00:18:39,224
the original one hundred thousand would have

598
00:18:39,224 --> 00:18:41,136
grown on its own to roughly four

599
00:18:41,136 --> 00:18:43,645
hundred thousand over twenty years at that

600
00:18:43,645 --> 00:18:44,780
same seven percent.

601
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:48,121
Together, that's roughly six hundred sixty thousand

602
00:18:48,121 --> 00:18:48,617
dollars.

603
00:18:48,797 --> 00:18:50,834
Again, that's an illustration,

604
00:18:50,834 --> 00:18:51,785
not a promise,

605
00:18:51,785 --> 00:18:54,093
but it shows why the habit matters

606
00:18:54,093 --> 00:18:56,266
more after one hundred thousand,

607
00:18:56,266 --> 00:18:56,877
not less.

608
00:18:57,057 --> 00:18:59,463
Try this: when you hit a milestone,

609
00:18:59,463 --> 00:19:03,107
raise your automatic contribution instead of lowering

610
00:19:03,107 --> 00:19:04,894
it, even by fifty dollars.

611
00:19:05,244 --> 00:19:06,358
Quick pause.

612
00:19:06,538 --> 00:19:07,851
If this is helping you,

613
00:19:07,851 --> 00:19:09,278
subscribe to the channel.

614
00:19:09,458 --> 00:19:11,215
It's free, and it helps me keep

615
00:19:11,215 --> 00:19:12,802
making these videos for you.

616
00:19:13,152 --> 00:19:15,033
Here's something most people miss.

617
00:19:15,213 --> 00:19:17,406
One hundred thousand dollars is not the

618
00:19:17,406 --> 00:19:18,812
same number at every age.

619
00:19:18,992 --> 00:19:20,199
The money is the same.

620
00:19:20,379 --> 00:19:22,446
What it can become is completely different.

621
00:19:22,626 --> 00:19:24,497
Let me show you three versions of

622
00:19:24,497 --> 00:19:25,575
the same milestone.

623
00:19:25,925 --> 00:19:27,062
Let's keep it simple.

624
00:19:27,242 --> 00:19:30,159
Imagine you reach one hundred thousand dollars

625
00:19:30,159 --> 00:19:32,632
invested, you never add another dollar,

626
00:19:32,632 --> 00:19:34,915
and it earns a seven percent average

627
00:19:34,915 --> 00:19:37,134
yearly return until age sixty five.

628
00:19:37,314 --> 00:19:38,931
That's an illustration,

629
00:19:38,931 --> 00:19:39,915
not a promise.

630
00:19:40,095 --> 00:19:41,976
But look at how much the starting

631
00:19:41,976 --> 00:19:42,661
age matters.

632
00:19:43,011 --> 00:19:44,456
Reach it at twenty five,

633
00:19:44,456 --> 00:19:46,504
and you have forty years of growth

634
00:19:46,504 --> 00:19:46,865
ahead.

635
00:19:47,045 --> 00:19:48,168
At seven percent,

636
00:19:48,168 --> 00:19:51,140
one hundred thousand dollars grows to roughly

637
00:19:51,140 --> 00:19:53,715
one point five million dollars by sixty

638
00:19:53,715 --> 00:19:56,159
five, without adding a single dollar.

639
00:19:56,339 --> 00:19:58,684
That is the most powerful position on

640
00:19:58,684 --> 00:20:00,839
this list, and almost nobody is in

641
00:20:00,839 --> 00:20:01,029
it.

642
00:20:01,380 --> 00:20:02,352
Reach it at forty,

643
00:20:02,352 --> 00:20:04,027
and you have twenty five years.

644
00:20:04,207 --> 00:20:06,316
The same one hundred thousand grows to

645
00:20:06,316 --> 00:20:08,758
roughly five hundred forty thousand dollars.

646
00:20:08,938 --> 00:20:10,517
Still a big number.

647
00:20:10,697 --> 00:20:12,626
Not a full retirement on its own

648
00:20:12,626 --> 00:20:13,590
for most people,

649
00:20:13,590 --> 00:20:15,097
but a serious foundation,

650
00:20:15,097 --> 00:20:17,207
especially if you keep adding to it

651
00:20:17,207 --> 00:20:17,930
every month.

652
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:19,638
Reach it at fifty five,

653
00:20:19,638 --> 00:20:20,996
and you have ten years.

654
00:20:21,176 --> 00:20:23,310
It grows to roughly one hundred ninety

655
00:20:23,310 --> 00:20:24,601
seven thousand dollars.

656
00:20:24,781 --> 00:20:26,545
That's still almost double,

657
00:20:26,545 --> 00:20:27,916
and it matters a lot.

658
00:20:28,096 --> 00:20:29,058
But at this age,

659
00:20:29,058 --> 00:20:30,621
the money you keep adding,

660
00:20:30,621 --> 00:20:32,485
and the years you keep working,

661
00:20:32,485 --> 00:20:34,168
matter more than the growth.

662
00:20:34,518 --> 00:20:35,707
So if you're young,

663
00:20:35,707 --> 00:20:37,083
the lesson is urgency.

664
00:20:37,263 --> 00:20:39,349
Every year you get there sooner is

665
00:20:39,349 --> 00:20:40,085
worth a lot.

666
00:20:40,265 --> 00:20:41,359
And if you're older,

667
00:20:41,359 --> 00:20:42,726
the lesson is not regret.

668
00:20:42,906 --> 00:20:43,800
It's focus.

669
00:20:43,980 --> 00:20:45,594
Let me explain what I mean.

670
00:20:45,944 --> 00:20:47,902
If you're in your twenties or early

671
00:20:47,902 --> 00:20:50,588
thirties, your biggest asset isn't your account.

672
00:20:50,768 --> 00:20:51,534
It's time.

673
00:20:51,714 --> 00:20:54,104
Cutting costs, raising your income,

674
00:20:54,104 --> 00:20:57,040
and automating your investing early all get

675
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:58,610
multiplied for decades.

676
00:20:58,790 --> 00:21:01,036
A raise you invest at twenty eight

677
00:21:01,036 --> 00:21:03,415
is worth far more by retirement than

678
00:21:03,415 --> 00:21:05,396
the same raise at forty eight.

679
00:21:05,747 --> 00:21:07,931
If you're in your forties or fifties,

680
00:21:07,931 --> 00:21:09,938
your biggest levers are different.

681
00:21:10,118 --> 00:21:11,950
Your income is usually higher,

682
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:13,905
so your savings rate can be much

683
00:21:13,905 --> 00:21:14,332
higher.

684
00:21:14,512 --> 00:21:15,826
In the United States,

685
00:21:15,826 --> 00:21:18,078
people fifty and older can also make

686
00:21:18,078 --> 00:21:21,330
extra catch up contributions to retirement accounts,

687
00:21:21,330 --> 00:21:23,707
so check the current limits each year.

688
00:21:23,887 --> 00:21:26,151
And lowering your fees and avoiding big

689
00:21:26,151 --> 00:21:28,706
mistakes protects what you've already built.

690
00:21:29,056 --> 00:21:30,225
And whatever your age,

691
00:21:30,225 --> 00:21:32,190
compare yourself to the right people.

692
00:21:32,370 --> 00:21:34,965
The Federal Reserve's survey found the median

693
00:21:34,965 --> 00:21:37,271
net worth for families under thirty five

694
00:21:37,271 --> 00:21:39,519
was about thirty nine thousand dollars,

695
00:21:39,519 --> 00:21:41,941
and about one hundred thirty five thousand

696
00:21:41,941 --> 00:21:44,189
for families thirty five to forty four.

697
00:21:44,369 --> 00:21:46,496
Hitting one hundred thousand early puts you

698
00:21:46,496 --> 00:21:47,040
well ahead.

699
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:49,716
Hitting it later is still real progress.

700
00:21:50,066 --> 00:21:52,283
The point isn't to feel good or

701
00:21:52,283 --> 00:21:53,642
bad about your age.

702
00:21:53,822 --> 00:21:55,969
It's to know which lever is strongest

703
00:21:55,969 --> 00:21:57,014
for you right now,

704
00:21:57,014 --> 00:21:58,407
and pull that one first.

705
00:21:58,758 --> 00:22:00,155
So how long does it take to

706
00:22:00,155 --> 00:22:02,226
get to one hundred thousand dollars from

707
00:22:02,226 --> 00:22:02,484
zero?

708
00:22:02,664 --> 00:22:04,279
It depends on two things:

709
00:22:04,279 --> 00:22:06,281
how much you invest each month,

710
00:22:06,281 --> 00:22:07,831
and what return you get.

711
00:22:08,011 --> 00:22:10,339
Let's look at a few realistic paths,

712
00:22:10,339 --> 00:22:13,119
with a seven percent average yearly return.

713
00:22:13,299 --> 00:22:15,793
Remember, real returns go up and down,

714
00:22:15,793 --> 00:22:17,630
and some years are negative.

715
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:20,187
At five hundred dollars a month and

716
00:22:20,187 --> 00:22:22,583
a seven percent average yearly return,

717
00:22:22,583 --> 00:22:24,980
it takes roughly eleven years to reach

718
00:22:24,980 --> 00:22:26,304
one hundred thousand.

719
00:22:26,484 --> 00:22:28,671
At one thousand dollars a month,

720
00:22:28,671 --> 00:22:30,652
roughly six and a half years.

721
00:22:30,832 --> 00:22:33,614
Doubling your contribution doesn't cut the time

722
00:22:33,614 --> 00:22:35,330
in half, but it gets close in

723
00:22:35,330 --> 00:22:36,277
the early years.

724
00:22:36,627 --> 00:22:38,415
And here's the encouraging part.

725
00:22:38,595 --> 00:22:40,706
The last stretch is faster than the

726
00:22:40,706 --> 00:22:41,068
first.

727
00:22:41,248 --> 00:22:43,355
With five hundred dollars a month,

728
00:22:43,355 --> 00:22:45,773
going from zero to fifty thousand takes

729
00:22:45,773 --> 00:22:47,447
about six and a half years.

730
00:22:47,627 --> 00:22:49,972
Going from fifty to one hundred thousand

731
00:22:49,972 --> 00:22:50,910
takes less time,

732
00:22:50,910 --> 00:22:53,079
because the money you already have is

733
00:22:53,079 --> 00:22:53,548
helping.

734
00:22:53,728 --> 00:22:55,005
That's compounding again.

735
00:22:55,185 --> 00:22:57,333
The hardest part really is the beginning.

736
00:22:57,684 --> 00:22:59,676
So if you're early in the journey

737
00:22:59,676 --> 00:23:00,763
and it feels slow,

738
00:23:00,763 --> 00:23:01,608
that's normal.

739
00:23:01,788 --> 00:23:03,762
It's supposed to feel slow at the

740
00:23:03,762 --> 00:23:04,121
start.

741
00:23:04,301 --> 00:23:05,288
Keep going.

742
00:23:05,639 --> 00:23:07,787
Let's make this concrete with three people.

743
00:23:07,967 --> 00:23:08,814
Same goal.

744
00:23:08,994 --> 00:23:10,480
Very different paths.

745
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:12,274
See which one sounds like you.

746
00:23:12,624 --> 00:23:13,239
Path one.

747
00:23:13,419 --> 00:23:14,846
Maya, twenty six,

748
00:23:14,846 --> 00:23:16,693
earns a modest salary.

749
00:23:16,873 --> 00:23:19,269
She can only invest three hundred dollars

750
00:23:19,269 --> 00:23:20,263
a month at first.

751
00:23:20,443 --> 00:23:21,816
But she automates it,

752
00:23:21,816 --> 00:23:24,104
never stops, and raises it by fifty

753
00:23:24,104 --> 00:23:26,457
dollars every time she gets a raise.

754
00:23:26,637 --> 00:23:28,994
She doesn't touch it during bad years.

755
00:23:29,174 --> 00:23:31,624
Her early progress feels painfully slow.

756
00:23:31,804 --> 00:23:33,610
It takes her years to reach the

757
00:23:33,610 --> 00:23:34,892
first twenty thousand.

758
00:23:35,072 --> 00:23:37,399
But because she started young and kept

759
00:23:37,399 --> 00:23:39,664
increasing, time does a huge share of

760
00:23:39,664 --> 00:23:40,215
her work.

761
00:23:40,395 --> 00:23:42,137
Her advantage isn't income.

762
00:23:42,317 --> 00:23:43,327
It's the calendar.

763
00:23:43,677 --> 00:23:44,362
Path two.

764
00:23:44,542 --> 00:23:46,153
Daniel, thirty four,

765
00:23:46,153 --> 00:23:48,408
earns more but started late.

766
00:23:48,588 --> 00:23:50,136
He had a car loan and credit

767
00:23:50,136 --> 00:23:51,850
card debt through his twenties.

768
00:23:52,030 --> 00:23:53,624
First he cleared the cards,

769
00:23:53,624 --> 00:23:56,102
which was like earning a guaranteed twenty

770
00:23:56,102 --> 00:23:56,988
percent return.

771
00:23:57,168 --> 00:24:00,032
Then he directed those same monthly payments,

772
00:24:00,032 --> 00:24:01,751
about nine hundred dollars,

773
00:24:01,751 --> 00:24:02,706
into investing.

774
00:24:02,886 --> 00:24:05,387
Because the habit of paying nine hundred

775
00:24:05,387 --> 00:24:06,888
a month already existed,

776
00:24:06,888 --> 00:24:08,702
he never felt the difference.

777
00:24:08,882 --> 00:24:10,915
His advantage is the size of his

778
00:24:10,915 --> 00:24:12,249
monthly contribution.

779
00:24:12,599 --> 00:24:13,423
Path three.

780
00:24:13,603 --> 00:24:14,626
Sara and Luis,

781
00:24:14,626 --> 00:24:16,599
a couple in their thirties,

782
00:24:16,599 --> 00:24:17,330
both work.

783
00:24:17,510 --> 00:24:19,446
They live on one income and invest

784
00:24:19,446 --> 00:24:20,470
most of the other.

785
00:24:20,650 --> 00:24:22,459
It's a big sacrifice for a few

786
00:24:22,459 --> 00:24:24,630
years, but it builds the cushion and

787
00:24:24,630 --> 00:24:26,560
the investments incredibly fast.

788
00:24:26,740 --> 00:24:29,066
They reach one hundred thousand in a

789
00:24:29,066 --> 00:24:31,650
few years, then relax their savings rate

790
00:24:31,650 --> 00:24:34,170
a bit, knowing the snowball is rolling.

791
00:24:34,350 --> 00:24:36,394
Their advantage is the savings rate.

792
00:24:36,744 --> 00:24:38,822
Notice what all three have in common.

793
00:24:39,002 --> 00:24:40,605
None of them got lucky with a

794
00:24:40,605 --> 00:24:41,324
single stock.

795
00:24:41,504 --> 00:24:43,113
None of them waited for a perfect

796
00:24:43,113 --> 00:24:43,454
moment.

797
00:24:43,634 --> 00:24:45,634
All three automated the habit,

798
00:24:45,634 --> 00:24:47,434
avoided high interest debt,

799
00:24:47,434 --> 00:24:49,567
and kept going during bad years.

800
00:24:49,747 --> 00:24:52,754
Time, contribution, savings rate.

801
00:24:52,934 --> 00:24:54,832
Pick the lever you can pull hardest,

802
00:24:54,832 --> 00:24:56,150
and pull it consistently.

803
00:24:56,500 --> 00:24:58,355
Here's a simple checklist you can do

804
00:24:58,355 --> 00:25:00,108
this week, wherever you are on the

805
00:25:00,108 --> 00:25:00,366
path.

806
00:25:00,546 --> 00:25:00,859
One.

807
00:25:01,039 --> 00:25:02,305
Calculate your net worth.

808
00:25:02,485 --> 00:25:05,522
Everything you own minus everything you owe,

809
00:25:05,522 --> 00:25:06,351
on one page.

810
00:25:06,531 --> 00:25:06,844
Two.

811
00:25:07,024 --> 00:25:08,879
Check that you have at least a

812
00:25:08,879 --> 00:25:11,167
few months of expenses in cash before

813
00:25:11,167 --> 00:25:12,156
you invest more.

814
00:25:12,336 --> 00:25:12,696
Three.

815
00:25:12,876 --> 00:25:15,129
List your debts by interest rate and

816
00:25:15,129 --> 00:25:17,195
decide which one to attack first.

817
00:25:17,545 --> 00:25:18,032
Four.

818
00:25:18,212 --> 00:25:19,362
Check your fees.

819
00:25:19,542 --> 00:25:22,112
Find the expense ratio of every fund

820
00:25:22,112 --> 00:25:24,611
you own and convert it into dollars

821
00:25:24,611 --> 00:25:25,254
per year.

822
00:25:25,434 --> 00:25:25,863
Five.

823
00:25:26,043 --> 00:25:27,925
Check your employer match and make sure

824
00:25:27,925 --> 00:25:29,132
you're getting all of it.

825
00:25:29,312 --> 00:25:29,776
Six.

826
00:25:29,956 --> 00:25:31,448
Set up, or raise,

827
00:25:31,448 --> 00:25:33,641
one automatic investment,

828
00:25:33,641 --> 00:25:35,308
even if it's small.

829
00:25:35,488 --> 00:25:38,258
And seven, check every beneficiary on every

830
00:25:38,258 --> 00:25:38,774
account.

831
00:25:39,124 --> 00:25:40,656
That's seven tasks.

832
00:25:40,836 --> 00:25:42,638
Most of them take less than half

833
00:25:42,638 --> 00:25:43,089
an hour.

834
00:25:43,269 --> 00:25:45,300
None of them require a big income.

835
00:25:45,480 --> 00:25:47,401
But together they remove most of the

836
00:25:47,401 --> 00:25:49,214
leaks that slow people down on the

837
00:25:49,214 --> 00:25:51,134
way to one hundred thousand dollars.

838
00:25:51,314 --> 00:25:52,104
Do them once,

839
00:25:52,104 --> 00:25:54,170
then check them again once a year.

840
00:25:54,521 --> 00:25:56,544
Five mistakes to avoid on the way

841
00:25:56,544 --> 00:25:58,015
to one hundred thousand.

842
00:25:58,195 --> 00:25:59,089
Mistake one.

843
00:25:59,269 --> 00:26:00,709
Chasing hot tips.

844
00:26:00,889 --> 00:26:03,111
Fast money stories are exciting,

845
00:26:03,111 --> 00:26:05,611
but most people build wealth slowly,

846
00:26:05,611 --> 00:26:08,180
with boring, diversified investments.

847
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:09,544
Mistake two.

848
00:26:09,724 --> 00:26:11,431
Stopping during a downturn.

849
00:26:11,611 --> 00:26:14,330
Contributions made when prices are low are

850
00:26:14,330 --> 00:26:16,661
often the ones that help most later.

851
00:26:17,011 --> 00:26:18,021
Mistake three.

852
00:26:18,201 --> 00:26:20,430
Ignoring fees, which we covered.

853
00:26:20,610 --> 00:26:23,025
Small percentages, big dollars.

854
00:26:23,205 --> 00:26:24,099
Mistake four.

855
00:26:24,279 --> 00:26:27,946
Skipping the emergency fund and investing everything,

856
00:26:27,946 --> 00:26:30,713
which turns every surprise into a forced

857
00:26:30,713 --> 00:26:31,059
sale.

858
00:26:31,239 --> 00:26:32,493
And mistake five.

859
00:26:32,673 --> 00:26:34,960
Comparing yourself with people online.

860
00:26:35,140 --> 00:26:36,210
You see their car,

861
00:26:36,210 --> 00:26:37,102
not their debt.

862
00:26:37,282 --> 00:26:38,426
Run your own race,

863
00:26:38,426 --> 00:26:39,825
with your own numbers.

864
00:26:40,175 --> 00:26:41,251
Before the recap,

865
00:26:41,251 --> 00:26:43,656
let me answer the questions people ask

866
00:26:43,656 --> 00:26:45,492
me most about this milestone.

867
00:26:45,842 --> 00:26:46,654
Question one.

868
00:26:46,834 --> 00:26:49,093
Does one hundred thousand dollars count if

869
00:26:49,093 --> 00:26:50,653
most of it is in a retirement

870
00:26:50,653 --> 00:26:51,084
account?

871
00:26:51,264 --> 00:26:51,705
Yes.

872
00:26:51,885 --> 00:26:54,114
Net worth counts everything you own,

873
00:26:54,114 --> 00:26:55,972
including retirement accounts.

874
00:26:56,152 --> 00:26:57,719
The difference is access.

875
00:26:57,899 --> 00:27:00,450
Money in a retirement account usually has

876
00:27:00,450 --> 00:27:03,623
rules and possible penalties for early withdrawals.

877
00:27:03,803 --> 00:27:05,700
So it counts for your net worth,

878
00:27:05,700 --> 00:27:07,657
but it's not your emergency fund.

879
00:27:08,007 --> 00:27:08,854
Question two.

880
00:27:09,034 --> 00:27:10,938
Should I pay off my mortgage or

881
00:27:10,938 --> 00:27:11,368
invest?

882
00:27:11,548 --> 00:27:13,276
It depends on your rate and your

883
00:27:13,276 --> 00:27:14,032
peace of mind.

884
00:27:14,212 --> 00:27:15,149
At a high rate,

885
00:27:15,149 --> 00:27:17,022
paying it down can make sense.

886
00:27:17,202 --> 00:27:18,520
At a low fixed rate,

887
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:21,420
many people prefer to invest the difference,

888
00:27:21,420 --> 00:27:24,584
because over long periods investments have often

889
00:27:24,584 --> 00:27:25,375
earned more.

890
00:27:25,555 --> 00:27:27,433
There's no single right answer,

891
00:27:27,433 --> 00:27:29,068
but there is a clear order:

892
00:27:29,068 --> 00:27:31,732
emergency fund and high interest debt first.

893
00:27:32,082 --> 00:27:33,115
Question three.

894
00:27:33,295 --> 00:27:34,955
Is it too late to start at

895
00:27:34,955 --> 00:27:35,977
forty, or fifty?

896
00:27:36,157 --> 00:27:36,319
No.

897
00:27:36,499 --> 00:27:38,913
You'll have fewer years for compounding,

898
00:27:38,913 --> 00:27:41,267
so the monthly contribution needs to do

899
00:27:41,267 --> 00:27:42,293
more of the work.

900
00:27:42,473 --> 00:27:44,628
But people in their forties and fifties

901
00:27:44,628 --> 00:27:46,895
are often at their highest earning years,

902
00:27:46,895 --> 00:27:47,558
which helps.

903
00:27:47,738 --> 00:27:49,726
Starting late is far better than not

904
00:27:49,726 --> 00:27:50,222
starting.

905
00:27:50,402 --> 00:27:51,447
Question four.

906
00:27:51,627 --> 00:27:53,462
Do I need a financial advisor?

907
00:27:53,642 --> 00:27:55,906
Not necessarily at this level.

908
00:27:56,086 --> 00:27:58,462
Many people do fine with simple low

909
00:27:58,462 --> 00:27:59,209
cost funds.

910
00:27:59,389 --> 00:28:00,915
If you do hire someone,

911
00:28:00,915 --> 00:28:02,906
know exactly how they're paid,

912
00:28:02,906 --> 00:28:05,029
and prefer someone who acts as a

913
00:28:05,029 --> 00:28:05,693
fiduciary.

914
00:28:06,043 --> 00:28:06,804
And one more,

915
00:28:06,804 --> 00:28:08,620
because it comes up every time.

916
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:11,250
Is one hundred thousand dollars enough to

917
00:28:11,250 --> 00:28:11,668
retire?

918
00:28:11,848 --> 00:28:12,705
On its own,

919
00:28:12,705 --> 00:28:14,576
no, not for most people.

920
00:28:14,756 --> 00:28:16,876
Using the four percent rule of thumb

921
00:28:16,876 --> 00:28:18,172
many planners mention,

922
00:28:18,172 --> 00:28:21,116
one hundred thousand dollars would support roughly

923
00:28:21,116 --> 00:28:23,707
four thousand dollars of withdrawals a year.

924
00:28:23,887 --> 00:28:25,650
That's a meaningful supplement,

925
00:28:25,650 --> 00:28:26,674
not a full income.

926
00:28:26,854 --> 00:28:29,136
Which is exactly why it's the starting

927
00:28:29,136 --> 00:28:30,697
line, not the finish line.

928
00:28:31,047 --> 00:28:32,266
Question six.

929
00:28:32,446 --> 00:28:34,781
What if the market crashes right after

930
00:28:34,781 --> 00:28:36,440
I hit one hundred thousand?

931
00:28:36,620 --> 00:28:38,918
It probably will drop at some point.

932
00:28:39,098 --> 00:28:41,949
Broad stock markets have fallen twenty percent

933
00:28:41,949 --> 00:28:43,809
or more many times in history,

934
00:28:43,809 --> 00:28:46,102
and they have always been scary while

935
00:28:46,102 --> 00:28:47,156
it was happening.

936
00:28:47,336 --> 00:28:48,848
If your money is invested,

937
00:28:48,848 --> 00:28:51,349
you might watch one hundred thousand become

938
00:28:51,349 --> 00:28:52,978
eighty thousand for a while.

939
00:28:53,158 --> 00:28:55,557
The people who did best historically were

940
00:28:55,557 --> 00:28:58,073
usually the ones who kept investing through

941
00:28:58,073 --> 00:29:00,063
the drop instead of selling at the

942
00:29:00,063 --> 00:29:00,472
bottom.

943
00:29:00,652 --> 00:29:03,576
Past results don't guarantee future ones,

944
00:29:03,576 --> 00:29:06,144
but panic selling locks in the loss.

945
00:29:06,494 --> 00:29:07,550
Question seven.

946
00:29:07,730 --> 00:29:09,437
Should I tell people I've reached it?

947
00:29:09,617 --> 00:29:12,258
Honestly, most people are better off keeping

948
00:29:12,258 --> 00:29:12,798
it quiet.

949
00:29:12,978 --> 00:29:14,424
Not because it's shameful,

950
00:29:14,424 --> 00:29:16,761
but because money changes how people treat

951
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:16,983
you.

952
00:29:17,163 --> 00:29:19,632
Friends may start asking for loans,

953
00:29:19,632 --> 00:29:21,255
family may expect help,

954
00:29:21,255 --> 00:29:24,006
and scammers love people they know have

955
00:29:24,006 --> 00:29:24,571
savings.

956
00:29:24,751 --> 00:29:26,968
Celebrate it with your partner or someone

957
00:29:26,968 --> 00:29:28,861
you trust, and let your habits stay

958
00:29:28,861 --> 00:29:29,348
the same.

959
00:29:29,698 --> 00:29:30,812
And question eight.

960
00:29:30,992 --> 00:29:33,349
What's the next milestone after this?

961
00:29:33,529 --> 00:29:35,497
For most people the next big one

962
00:29:35,497 --> 00:29:37,835
is two hundred fifty thousand dollars,

963
00:29:37,835 --> 00:29:39,311
and then half a million.

964
00:29:39,491 --> 00:29:41,232
Here's the encouraging part.

965
00:29:41,412 --> 00:29:43,130
At a seven percent return,

966
00:29:43,130 --> 00:29:46,037
money roughly doubles about every ten years,

967
00:29:46,037 --> 00:29:47,821
by the rule of seventy two.

968
00:29:48,001 --> 00:29:50,323
So the second one hundred thousand usually

969
00:29:50,323 --> 00:29:51,871
comes faster than the first,

970
00:29:51,871 --> 00:29:53,861
because your money is now doing part

971
00:29:53,861 --> 00:29:54,967
of the work for you.

972
00:29:55,317 --> 00:29:56,902
If you have a question I didn't

973
00:29:56,902 --> 00:29:58,846
answer here, leave it in the comments.

974
00:29:59,026 --> 00:29:59,881
I read them,

975
00:29:59,881 --> 00:30:02,660
and the best ones become future videos.

976
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:04,643
And if you're still far from one

977
00:30:04,643 --> 00:30:07,177
hundred thousand, don't let that number scare

978
00:30:07,177 --> 00:30:07,403
you.

979
00:30:07,583 --> 00:30:09,882
Every person who reached it started at

980
00:30:09,882 --> 00:30:12,061
zero, with a first deposit that felt

981
00:30:12,061 --> 00:30:13,272
too small to matter.

982
00:30:13,452 --> 00:30:14,334
It did matter.

983
00:30:14,514 --> 00:30:16,124
It was the start of the habit,

984
00:30:16,124 --> 00:30:17,733
and the habit is what gets you

985
00:30:17,733 --> 00:30:18,055
there.

986
00:30:18,235 --> 00:30:20,138
So set up the transfer today,

987
00:30:20,138 --> 00:30:21,385
even if it's small,

988
00:30:21,385 --> 00:30:23,550
and let time do the heavy lifting

989
00:30:23,550 --> 00:30:24,075
for you.

990
00:30:24,425 --> 00:30:26,677
Here's a quick reminder of how small

991
00:30:26,677 --> 00:30:28,117
that first step can be.

992
00:30:28,297 --> 00:30:30,063
Two hundred dollars a month,

993
00:30:30,063 --> 00:30:31,135
for twenty years,

994
00:30:31,135 --> 00:30:33,722
at a seven percent average yearly return,

995
00:30:33,722 --> 00:30:36,372
grows to roughly one hundred four thousand

996
00:30:36,372 --> 00:30:36,876
dollars.

997
00:30:37,056 --> 00:30:38,891
You'd put in forty eight thousand.

998
00:30:39,071 --> 00:30:42,447
Small, boring, and automatic beats big and

999
00:30:42,447 --> 00:30:43,332
occasional.

1000
00:30:43,682 --> 00:30:44,494
Quick recap.

1001
00:30:44,674 --> 00:30:47,054
One, compounding becomes visible.

1002
00:30:47,234 --> 00:30:49,234
Two, your money can out earn your

1003
00:30:49,234 --> 00:30:49,719
savings.

1004
00:30:49,899 --> 00:30:52,621
Three, the rule of seventy two gets

1005
00:30:52,621 --> 00:30:53,010
real.

1006
00:30:53,190 --> 00:30:55,071
Four, you think in net worth.

1007
00:30:55,251 --> 00:30:57,387
Five, fees start to hurt.

1008
00:30:57,567 --> 00:31:00,516
Six, allocation beats stock picking.

1009
00:31:00,696 --> 00:31:02,740
Seven, drops feel real.

1010
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:04,975
Eight, taxes matter more.

1011
00:31:05,325 --> 00:31:08,738
Nine, your emergency fund guards your investments.

1012
00:31:08,918 --> 00:31:11,252
Ten, home equity isn't cash.

1013
00:31:11,432 --> 00:31:14,717
Eleven, high interest debt becomes an emergency.

1014
00:31:14,897 --> 00:31:17,173
Twelve, you become a sales target.

1015
00:31:17,353 --> 00:31:19,640
Thirteen, the paperwork matters.

1016
00:31:19,820 --> 00:31:22,444
Fourteen, your options multiply.

1017
00:31:22,624 --> 00:31:24,853
And fifteen, the trap of easing off

1018
00:31:24,853 --> 00:31:27,337
right when the snowball starts rolling.

1019
00:31:27,688 --> 00:31:29,449
If you want the full step by

1020
00:31:29,449 --> 00:31:31,650
step plan, from zero to one hundred

1021
00:31:31,650 --> 00:31:32,908
thousand and beyond,

1022
00:31:32,908 --> 00:31:34,166
it's in my playbook.

1023
00:31:34,346 --> 00:31:35,902
The link is in the description.

1024
00:31:36,082 --> 00:31:37,198
And if you missed it,

1025
00:31:37,198 --> 00:31:38,952
watch my video on what changes at

1026
00:31:38,952 --> 00:31:40,865
twenty thousand dollars in the bank.

1027
00:31:41,045 --> 00:31:43,255
That's the foundation for everything we talked

1028
00:31:43,255 --> 00:31:43,831
about today.

1029
00:31:44,011 --> 00:31:45,265
I'm Ryan Rover.

1030
00:31:45,445 --> 00:31:46,641
See you in the next one.
